# a-base-the-player-builds — plan 27 C5, and it is HALF A PAIR.
#
# `a-base-nobody-builds.keys` and `a-base-the-player-builds.keys` are
# the same file except for ONE token: `paint off` against `paint on`.
# Same ground, same core, same spawn, same wave list, same crew, same
# drive, same number of ticks spent before the wave — so the difference
# in the clock is the WALL and can be nothing else.
#
# ⚠ That is `plans/12` § B7's rule, and § Invariant gate names the
# failure it guards: *equal times = the scenario measures nothing,
# whatever it draws*.
#
# ── What the player does ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Parks at (5, -2), presses paint, and drives to (5, 2) — which orders
# FIVE hexes: the one it was standing on when the mode came on, plus the
# four it enters.  That is the band's whole walkable height, so the wall
# SEALS; `plans/12` § B7 measured that one with a gate in it buys
# nothing at all.  Two helpers sit on the line and raise them —
# `numbers.json` § wall.build_time_wall is 10 s a hex for one helper, so
# five hexes is 50 helper-seconds and the pair need 25 s.
#
# ⚠⚠ **The recon window is what pays for it** (`@X022`): the wave list
# does not start until the player pokes a spawn marker, so the ticks
# spent building are free — which is the design claim this pair makes
# measurable for the first time.

echo the player builds: a wall drawn by driving, raised by the crew

do toggle_mode

do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 11 0
marker 11 0 spawn 3

count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag -3 -2 12 -2
drag -3 -1 12 -1
drag -3 0 12 0
drag -3 1 12 1
drag -3 2 12 2
count painted 80 80

# The crew, sitting on the line the player is about to draw.
crew 5 -1
crew 5 1

# ── The build (or not) ──────────────────────────────────────────
park 5 -2
paint on
drive 5 2
tick 6
count orders 5 5
paint off

# Park clear of the line so the trail is not rubbed out, and let the
# crew work.  60 helper-seconds over two helpers is 30 s = 45 ticks.
drive 0 0
tick 50
count orders 0 0
count painted 80 80
# ⚠⚠ The whole band, hex by hex — because a wall with ONE gap in it
# buys nothing at all (`plans/12` § B7 measured a gate at +1 tick), so
# *five* is the number that matters rather than *some*.
kind 5 -2 wall
kind 5 -1 wall
kind 5 0 wall
kind 5 1 wall
kind 5 2 wall


# ── The waves — identical to `an-undefended-base` ───────────────
wave 5
count alive 5 5

tick 22
wave 8
count alive 13 13

# ── The clock ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠⚠ **130 against 174 — the wall the player DREW is worth +44 ticks,
# a third of the base's life** (`@M050`).  Same ground, same core, same
# spawn, same wave list, same crew, same drive, same ticks spent — one
# token apart.
fall 900
ticks 170 178
snap c5_a_base_the_player_builds
